# Cloudflare Pages headers. There is no build step, so nothing here is
# content-hashed — every URL keeps its name across deploys. That single fact
# decides every rule below.
#
# The failure this file exists to prevent: a coach opens the app on Saturday
# and gets a version from three weeks ago because an intermediary is still
# holding `index.html` or `sw.js`. `sw.js` is the worst of the two — it is the
# only thing that can replace itself, so a stale copy is stale forever.
#
# The other half of the trap runs the other way: a file cached long here is a
# file the worker can re-install *stale*. So nothing that appears in `PRECACHE`
# gets a long cache. Cloudflare Pages' own default for uncached assets is
# `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, which is already correct for those; the
# rules below are the deliberate exceptions and the two belt-and-braces ones.
#
# **Be precise about how the worker actually installs**, because two earlier
# versions of this comment were wrong about it and one of them invited a rewrite
# that would have broken offline boot. `sw.js` does NOT use `cache.addAll` — see
# the long comment above `precache()`, which explains that `addAll` stores a
# redirected response and the spec then refuses to serve it for a navigation.
# And `precache()` fetches with `{ cache: 'reload' }`, which BYPASSES the HTTP
# cache, so no `max-age` set here can stale an install.
#
# The policy still stands, for the paths that do read this file:
#   - the first, uncontrolled load, before any worker exists;
#   - `index.html` and `sw.js` themselves, above;
#   - `LAZY` (the Inter ext subset), which the fetch handler gets with a plain
#     `fetch(req)` — the one file that really is served out of the HTTP cache
#     and then written into the worker's cache.

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/about.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Never cache the updater. A stale service worker cannot be replaced by a
# newer one it never fetches.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Inter is in the worker's cache either way — the latin subset precached, the
# ext subset fetched on demand — so this is a week rather than a year: long
# enough that no coach refetches it, short enough that a re-vendor is not
# frozen into returning browsers past the next SHELL change.
/vendor/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security headers, on every path. Nothing below costs the app anything: `app/`
# contains no iframe, no `<object>` or `<embed>`, no `<base>`, and nothing
# cross-origin that needs a referrer path.
#
# **Be honest about what the CSP buys.** It carries `'unsafe-inline'` and
# therefore does NOT stop injected inline script: counted 2026-08-24, there are
# 15 inline `<script>` blocks across the 8 HTML files (8 of them the pre-paint
# theme resolver, duplicated on purpose rather than shared) and 264 inline
# `style=` attributes. Hashing them instead would want a build step, and a build
# step is rejected: it would invalidate this file, the whole `dead-*` suite and
# `sw.test.js`'s precache-vs-import-graph check.
#
# What it DOES close is the realistic supply-chain risk. There is exactly one
# third-party script on the entire site — the Cloudflare beacon, injected at
# `analytics.js:163` — and there is no `eval` and no `new Function` anywhere,
# `vendor/motion.umd.js` included. `script-src` names that one origin and locks
# out every other. `object-src` and `base-uri` close the two classic ways an
# inline injection escalates; `frame-ancestors` is the clickjacking half and
# supersedes `X-Frame-Options`.
#
# There is deliberately no `default-src`. It would silently govern `img-src`,
# `font-src`, `connect-src` and `manifest-src` as well, and a policy that can
# break the card offline in a gym buys nothing here that `script-src` has not
# already bought.
#
# HSTS is belt-and-braces, not a hole being closed: `.app` is an HSTS-preloaded
# TLD, so every modern browser already refuses plain HTTP to this host before a
# request leaves it. It is here because it is one line and it covers the
# browsers and preview hostnames that preloading does not. No `includeSubDomains`
# — there are no subdomains to speak for.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), display-capture=()
  Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000
